sec.p:
The physiological role of basophils is mainly in the defence against parasites such as helminths, but studies in mouse models also suggest that they play essential roles in the defence against ticks (
Haemaphysalis longicornis
) [7, 8]. Besides this and their role in allergic diseases, basophils are involved in a variety of other pathologies including cancer, myocardial infarction, autoimmunity, fibrosis, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease [9, 10]. Activated basophils have been observed in the blood of patients with chronic urticaria and systemic lupus erythematosus. Additionally, IgE autoantibodies and activated basophils are factors associated with disease activity in lupus nephritis [11, 12]. Basophils in the circulation and within the tumour microenvironment (TME) may also contribute to anti‐tumour immunity and patient outcomes [13].
